          "text": "Leo, an exact replacement can safely tolerate unrelated file changes. It checks that the targeted text still matches; it does not prove the surrounding document still means the same thing. High confidence.\n\n| Harness | Edit contract |\n|---|---|\n| **H1: My `apply_patch` tool** | Diff-like hunks containing context, removed lines, and added lines. The public Codex matcher also tolerates some whitespace and punctuation differences, so it is not strictly byte-exact. It does not guarantee contextual readback afterward. [Source](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/apply-patch/src/seek_sequence.rs) |\n| **H2: OpenCode** | `oldString` → `newString`. Its newer editor requires an exact match, rejects ambiguity unless `replaceAll` is explicit, and checks for intervening changes before writing. Its older implementation also has fuzzy fallbacks. [Source](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/337fd144d2ba144743368f78d9579a99cce175bd/packages/core/src/tool/edit.ts) |\n| **H3: OMP** | Defaults to snapshot-tagged line operations. It tracks observed ranges and attempts recovery of stale anchors; successful edits return a fresh tag and compact preview. More machinery than simple replacement. [Source](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/1adcef9762b065c0cef15703fd3e78ecaaa52a3b/docs/tools/edit.md) |\n\nFor llame, I recommend the simplest exact replacement:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"path\": \"projects/llame.md\",\n  \"oldText\": \"Monthly budget: 100\",\n  \"newText\": \"Monthly budget: 150\"\n}\n```\n\nIf another agent changed a different paragraph, this still applies to the **current file**, preserving that change. If the target changed or appears twice, refuse and request more context. Keep matching and writing under the same file lock for cooperating writers. That removes the need for my earlier whole-file freshness rejection on `edit`.\n\nYour context rule is clear:\n\n- **D9 — Read:** requesting displayed lines 10–12 returns lines 9–13 when available.\n- **D10 — Edit:** return the resulting changed region, plus one current line before and after. A deletion returns the surviving context around the deletion.\n- **D11 — Truncation:** the underlying tool adds those neighbors; the common result handler applies ordinary truncation. No special budget or guaranteed preservation for context lines.\n\nThose neighbors are display context, not additional edit preconditions unless included in `oldText`. Continuation should advance through the requested range without counting the extra trailing context as requested content.\n\n**Q6 — What should whole-file `write` do when the file already exists?** Unlike `edit`, it has no old section to match.\n\nMy recommendation: allow creation; allow replacement only if the file is unchanged since the agent's read. Example: B edits any part after A reads; A's whole-file `write` fails, but A's exact nonconflicting `edit` can still succeed. Does that distinction fit?\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:127-134|note=[located prior ranged-read research; current sources and new user requirements take precedence]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a042d2-65e5-7db1-b139-ed2a1d29139c\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>",
