          "text": "Leo, OMP's current default uses **snapshot-tagged line edits**. You reference what you read and supply only the new content.\n\nFor example, a read could show:\n\n```text\n[notes.md#A1B2]\n1:# Project\n2:\n3:Monthly budget: 100\n4:Review date: September\n5:\n6:Owner: Leo\n```\n\nTo change the budget, the agent sends this to `edit`:\n\n```text\n[notes.md#A1B2]\nPUT 3.=3:\n+Monthly budget: 150\n```\n\n`A1B2` identifies the recorded snapshot. `3.=3` selects line 3 in that snapshot. The agent doesn't repeat the old text or supply a whole-file hash.\n\nOther operations use the same format:\n\n| Operation | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Replace lines 3–4 | `PUT 3.=4:` followed by new `+` lines |\n| Insert after line 4 | `PUT >4:` followed by new `+` lines |\n| Append | `PUT >$:` followed by new `+` lines |\n| Delete lines 3–4 | `CUT 3.=4` |\n\nOMP also supports syntax-aware blocks. In Markdown, targeting a heading with `PUT N*:` replaces its section, including deeper subsections up to the next heading of equal or higher level. [OMP edit documentation](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/1adcef9762b065c0cef15703fd3e78ecaaa52a3b/docs/tools/edit.md)\n\nIf another agent changes the file after the read, OMP attempts recovery against its recorded snapshots. It refuses when it cannot establish a unique safe application. Edits outside the ranges actually exposed to the agent are also rejected. A successful edit returns a fresh snapshot tag and a compact post-edit preview when available. [Validation and recovery](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/1adcef9762b065c0cef15703fd3e78ecaaa52a3b/docs/tools/edit.md#limits-and-validation)\n\nThe appeal is concrete: **less old text to reproduce, compact edits, and recovery when line positions move**. The cost is a snapshot store, observed-range tracking, and recovery logic behind the simple interface. High confidence in these mechanics; whether they outperform exact replacement for llame needs a comparison.",
